Effects of additive manufacturing on convective heat transfer in 3D-printed micro/mini-channels and fluid passages with micro/mini structures The review
Tomasz Bulsiewicz , Piotr Łapka *
Faculty of Power and Aeronautical Engineering, Warsaw University of Technology, Nowowiejska St. 21/25, 00-665 Warsaw, Poland
ARTICLE INFO
Keywords:
Additive manufacturing Convective heat transfer Friction factor Heat transfer coefficient Micro/mini-channels Micro/mini structures Pressure drop
ABSTRACT
Additive manufacturing is a novel method of producing three-dimensional products. It allows designers to break boundaries in terms of the complexity of the geometries produced, in many cases reducing the number of parts in the final component and enabling more cost-effective production. Such an advantage of the additive manufacturing process is very attractive, especially for heat transfer designs where cooling efficiency can be boosted by developing complex fluid flow paths, and the design space of possible solutions is extended significantly. As 3D printing advances as a manufacturing method, it is crucial to understand its inherent limitations, especially in developing micro-channel structures. This paper presents a comprehensive review of the literature on heat transfer and flow phenomena in 3D-printed micro/mini-channels and fluid passages with micro/mini structures, with a particular focus on surface roughness. This has an impact on friction factors and, consequently, on pressure drops and, according to the Reynolds analogy, also on heat transfer. It is important to understand how the printing strategy can impact the thermal performance of the components. This review will, therefore, focus on the various heat transfer augmentation features and additive manufacturing factors.
1. Introduction
Additive manufacturing, also known as 3D printing, is an advanced manufacturing technology that creates 3D objects by successive addition of material layers [1]. The advantages and capabilities of additive manufacturing have been utilized in many industries as it allows for the development of new products with significantly improved properties, functionalities, and efficiencies at reduced production costs. Advances in this production technology and the recent explosion of materials and methods for developing additively manufactured devices have opened a new era in thermal management [2]. Novel and miniaturized additively manufactured heat exchangers, as well as cooling and heating systems with 3D-printed parts, are finding applications in many industries, e.g., in electronics, automotive, aerospace, gas turbine, medicine, construction, etc. [2]. The 3D printing technology allows engineers to expand the realm of spatial and surface design significantly. This technology demonstrated great improvement in production process automation, a high level of design freedom, the ability to manufacture complex structures, the great potential for customization, optimization, and material savings, and the ability for fast prototyping. Therefore, in the
novel design of heat exchangers and cooling/heating systems, the optimization of convective heat transfer, and significant miniaturization and compactness of products, can be achieved. In these products, functional fluid channels may exhibit very sophisticated shapes and flow path arrangements, which lead to highly extended heat transfer area and high intensification (turbulisation) of fluid flow [2]. Furthermore, 3D printing may simplify the manufacturing process of products with complex, internal fluid channels, which are frequently produced from many parts, into one component. In consequence, in such products with sophisticated fluid channels, which can operate at high-pressure ratios, leakages at the joints can be eliminated, improving the overall performance of the part while maintaining safety and service life. These complex, compact, and efficient products produced with additive manufacturing technology might not be easily obtained by applying traditional manufacturing techniques. Additive manufacturing technology has a lot of undoubted advantages. However, there are still many challenges in designing and manufacturing 3D-printed products with functional fluid channels and streamlined bodies, especially at the mini and micro scale [3]. These challenges are mainly related to process repeatability and quality. In 3D
