printing technology, it is not easy to precisely control the quality of the formed surface, which will be in contact with a fluid. This includes surface roughness and contour accuracy, which greatly influence the fluid flow structure and heat transfer characteristics [3], especially for smaller hydraulic diameters. On the one hand, a rough surface can augment heat transfer by a trip of a boundary layer in convective heat transport. But on the other hand, it leads to an increase of the pressure drop (loss) across the dissipation region. Therefore, the 3D-printing may provide both opportunities related to increasing heat transfer efficiency and threads associated with channel cross-section shape accuracy and an increase in the pressure drop along the flow path. The 3D printing technology shows much promise for use in many industries. However, understanding the fluid flow and heat transfer phenomena in parts made with additive manufacturing is crucial for successfully utilizing this technology for certain applications. To date, the majority of research and development efforts in the field of additively manufactured heat exchangers or components of cooling/heating systems have been oriented toward achieving superior thermal performance over devices produced by means of traditional manufactured technologies [4–6]. These researches mainly focused on developing sophisticated geometries, which lead to improved heat transfer performance. However, there are also several studies in which the effects of internal microstructure and surface morphology of additively manufactured parts are studied regarding their influence on the heat transfer and fluid flow phenomena. Some of these studies showed that selected 3D printing process parameters, e.g., building direction or laser scanning parameters, greatly influence surface roughness and related heat transfer. Moreover, investigations were conducted that quantify and qualify the influence of additive manufacturing process parameters and related internal microstructure and surface morphology on the fluid flow and heat transfer. The goal of this review is to focus on a basic research summary for understanding the limitations of additive manufacturing technology in aspects of repeatability of the process, an appropriate representation of manufactured geometries, and parameters, which can influence the accuracy of the process in terms of roughness and its impact to convective heat transfer and pressure drop. Such knowledge is crucial for developing 3D-printed components for efficient thermal management in many devices and systems.
2. Surface roughness and shape characterization in additively manufactured elements
Incompressible flows characterization and the impact of surface roughness was the area of research from the beginning of the 20th century. In the literature, many theoretical models exist that allow the prediction of friction factors based on relative surface roughness. The friction factor is important in assessing pressure drop in manufactured channels. For the first time, the term relative surface roughness was used by Kohlrausch [7], who defined the ratio of wall roughness to the hydraulic diameter of the channel. The surface roughness of the wall is known as the average value of roughness (Ra) and can be obtained by means, e.g., profilometry measurement. Extensive experiments were conducted by Nikuradse [8], who tried to characterize flow for a broad
range of Reynolds (Re) numbers from 600 to 106. Nikuradse experiments were conducted on pipes with a diameter on a macroscale from 25 to 100 mm. The main conclusion was that roughnesses do not impact the friction factor in the laminar and transition regime of the flow. This experimental work is dated to the 1933 year. However, it should be noted that Nikurades [8] did not perform any heat transfer measurements in sand roughened tubes. Researchers came back to this topic at the end of the 20th century and started investigating microscale (channels from 100 to 1000 μm range) and see if the theory developed by Nikuradse is still valid. For example, a summary of new information can be found in [9–12]. Firstly, the rise in the friction factor relates to an increase in the surface roughness. Additionally, researchers observed
that the transition zone between laminar and turbulent flow, could appear earlier with increased surface roughness. These findings are very important to 3D-printing technology. All the benefits that additive manufacturing can bring to engineers, especially the desire for an increase in cooling effectiveness of designed parts in many situations by means of using microscale cooling features, make the topic of surface topology characterization and understanding extremely important to understand the limitations of this manufacturing method better. One of the most popular methods for additive manufacturing is the Direct Metal Laser Sintering (DMLS), which belongs to the family of Powder Bed Fusion (PBF) methods. The production process in PBF methods starts by supplying powder from which the part will be manufactured into the bed, which is moving in the radial direction. Next, the laser provides a high heat flux in a direction based on a digital model of the part, which sinters powder in the intended shape. After solidifying the first layer, the bed drops and refills the amount of powder using a recoater, and the process starts again until the whole part is finished. In this method, obvious parameters influencing the final part shape and, as a consequence, surface roughness can be the size of the bed movement (layer thickness), laser beam spot size and energy, and powder particle size. It was observed that 3D printing process parameters greatly influence surface morphology and related surface roughness. Two primary modes for roughness generation were identified [13]. They are the staircase effect and the dross formation at downward-facing surfaces due to gravity forces, as is shown in Fig. 1. The staircase effect corresponds to layer-by-layer additive manufacturing practices and is directly related to the layer height and the discrete partitioning of the slicing conducted prior to the manufacturing process [13]. The effect of additive manufacturing parameters on surface morphology was investigated by Fox et al. [14]. They built samples covering a range of overhang angles and process parameters in the Laser Powder Bed Fusion (LPBF) system. Analysis was then performed to determine a relationship between process parameters, i.e., beam power, beam velocity, overhang angle, and surface roughness. In turn, Klingaa et al. [13] carried out a complete roughness characterization of circular 17–4 PH stainless steel channels manufactured by the LPBF. Samples were produced with different global and local building orientation angles. The investigation confirmed that the roughness was highly dependent on both the global and the local orientation. Studies related to the control of the boundary slope of optimized parts in density-based topology optimization for additive manufacturing were carried out by Wang et al. [15]. The boundary slope of a component affects the amount of support structure required during its fabrication by additive processes and is directly related to the resulting surface roughness, which in turn affects the heat transfer efficiency. By constraining the minimal boundary slope, support structures can be eliminated or reduced, while by constraining the maximal boundary slope, high surface roughness can be attained, and thus, the heat transfer efficiency may be increased. The proposed control strategy was applied to various examples, including linear elasticity, heat transfer, and thermoelastic problems. Experimentally printed test coupons confirmed the ability of the proposed strategy to surface roughness control of the printed parts. Oter et al. [16] investigated the impact of placing the additively manufactured parts on the build platform. They concluded that location and laser angles connected to this location influence the final surface roughness of the parts. They also observed that the amount of support structures has an impact on the morphology of the surface, which can relate to the intensity of heat transfer processes (i.e., increased heat conductive effect through support structures) during part manufacturing. Snyder et al. [17] studied build direction on the shape of the microchannels. During the investigation, three build directions were investigated: vertical, horizontal, and angled (i.e., 0◦, 90◦, and 45◦ angles to the build direction). The conclusion was that the unsupported surface of the
