part was always deformed and had the highest friction factor value. Shape modification can be implemented during the design phase to compensate for these deformations. Such a check was done by Snyder et al. [18]. Very similar results were obtained by Wildgoose et al. [19]. During this study, five buildup angles were checked, i.e., 0◦, 30◦, 45◦, 60◦, and 90◦, and three diameters of cylindrical channels (i.e., 750, 1000, and 1250 μm). For coupons with angles below 60◦, the shape of the cylindrical section of the channel was deformed, regardless of the size of the channel. For 60◦ and 90◦, the coupons’ surface roughness changed minimally regardless of the diameter of the channel. In terms of surface roughness, it increased with the decrease in buildup angle. Wildgoose et al. [20] studied the impact of build layer thickness and wall thickness of the channel on the surface roughness of the produced part. During the study, a couple of turbine airfoil geometries were printed with varying wall thicknesses and build layer thicknesses (i.e., 40 and 80 μm). The main conclusions were that for thinner walls, especially below 600 μm, the surface roughness started to rise significantly. Additionally, the surface roughness of printed elements was higher for thicker building layers, which corresponds to the staircase effect during the manufacturing process (see Fig. 1). This quick review shows the importance of knowing factors that can impact additive manufactured design by engineers. When deciding on additive manufacturing, the designer should plan out a strategy for choosing the appropriate angle of the print and incorporate corrections for shape change.
3. Convective heat transfer and friction factors characteristics in additively manufactured micro/mini channels
In the area of convective heat transfer analysis in the 3D-printed micro/mini-channels and around 3D-printed micro/mini streamlined bodies, few studies were carried out in terms of the roughness or different additive manufacturing process effects parameters on pressure losses and heat transfer augmentation. The first paper, which investigated the effect of roughness on convective heat transfer in the 3Dprinted flat and finned heat sinks, was published by Ventola et al. [21]. They studied external flow over fins manufactured with the DMLS. They found that the roughness significantly augments the heat transfer on rough flat surfaces, i.e., peaks of 73 % and 63 % on average were
observed for the convective heat transfer enhancement compared to smooth surfaces. These results refer to Reynolds numbers (on heated edge) within the range of 3500–16,500. One of the first papers devoted to the examination of the effect of build direction on the quality of internal micro-channels (i.e., the hydraulic diameter of 508 μm) and resulting friction factor and heat transfer was published by Snyder et al. [18]. In this work, test coupons made of Inconel 718 were produced by applying the DMLS process, more generally referred to as Selective Laser Melting (SLM). Three different build directions were studied (vertical, 45◦, and horizontal), with the Reynolds number ranging from 200 up to 12,000. Flow and heat transfer tests revealed a significant influence of build direction on pressure loss, while additive manufacturing process parameters did not significantly affect thermal performance. It was found that vertically built coupons had the lowest friction factor, while the diagonally built coupons had the highest friction factor. Similar investigations but for rectangular micro/mini-channels were performed by Stimpson et al. [22] with the only effect of roughness investigated, while 3D printing process parameters were not studied. The Reynolds number range examined was from approx. 1000 up to 50,000. While high augmentations in flow intensity resulted in increased pressure losses, the Nusselt number augmentations did not linearly scale with the friction factors increase. In addition, both works [18,22] demonstrated that correlations for friction factor, e.g., Nikuradse [8], and heat transfer coefficient, e.g., Dittus-Boelter, for smooth and rough surfaces no longer hold true for additively manufactured surfaces with high roughness values. In the next study, Stimpson et al. [23] focused on characterizing the morphology and quantifying the roughness of micro-channel surfaces made with the DMLS. They developed correlations that relate the roughness measurements to the flow friction and heat transfer for Reynolds numbers up to 50,000, which gives better predictions than correlations for a smooth surface. Parent-Simard et al. [24] presented experimental and analytical studies of the influence of surface roughness on the performances of a 3D-printed, counterflow, mini-channel heat exchanger. Results showed that the tolerances of the process, which was at ±100 μm, reduced the mini-channels’ hydraulic diameter (due to roughness and shape inaccuracy) and increased the pressure drop. They recommended adjusting additive manufacturing process parameters to tighten the process tolerances for cases where important dimension deviations severely affect
