the design’s hydraulic and thermal performance. Recently, Snyder and Thole [25] investigated opportunities to tailor additively manufactured surfaces through control of the process parameters to enhance pressure loss and heat transfer characteristics in the internal and external cooling geometries applied in the turbine systems. Test coupons with mini/ micro-channels were additively manufactured by applying the LPBF with different laser powers, scan speeds, and scanning strategies and were examined in the Reynolds number range of 1000–12,000. Experimental results showed that the process parameters significantly impact the surface morphology, leading to differences in cooling performance. Moreover, the research showed the great potential of manipulating the parameters within the additive manufacturing process to control the surface morphology, directly influencing the fluid flow and heat transfer characteristics. In a recent paper, Flys et al. [26] evaluated the effect of surface roughness and channel dimensions on the pressure drop and heat transfer in the conventional cooling channels produced by the PBF using the laser beam melting additive manufacturing technique. Compared to the previously mentioned papers, Flys et al. [26] consider conventional channels of a quite high diameter equal to approx. 10 mm instead of mini/micro-channels with diameters smaller than 1000 μm. In the first step of the research, cooling channels with as-build surfaces were compared to post-processed cooling channels such as extrude honing and drilled channels, respectively, considering flows with Reynolds numbers up to 100,000 and water as a working medium. In the second step, the influence of the surface topology on the pressure drops and heat transfer was investigated by applying typical parameters characterizing the surface. They concluded the research that the complexity of surface topology of as-build channels needs to be described by parameters suitable for the detection of fluid interaction. They also suggested further examination of the combination of effects of different manufacturing process parameters on pressure drop and heat transfer. Simple circular channels printed on a microscale are not the only interest of research groups. As mentioned above, additive manufacturing allows for overcoming the limitations of the shape of the channels, which cannot be achieved with conventional manufacturing techniques. An example of such a study is the work of Wildgoose et al. [27]. Nine channels with different shapes (i.e., circular, hexagonal, pentagonal, elliptical, diamond, square, rectangular, trapezoidal, and triangular cross-sections) were manufactured using the DMLS process from Inco 718 material and the same build direction (90◦ angle). To compare cooling efficiency and quantify the impact of additive manufacturing, the same cross-sectional perimeter was chosen, and the hydraulic diameter of all channels varied from around 1000 to 1500 μm. Pressure drop and heat transfer tests were performed in a broad range of Reynolds numbers from 500 up to 50,000. The paper showed that all coupons were manufactured with a bigger cross-sectional area regardless of the channel shape. Perimeters were closer to the design intent. Friction factors compared to the smooth channel for the range of Reynolds number varied from 2 up to 4 times, and heat transfer augmentation also compared to smooth channel using Dittus-Boelter equation were higher from 20 to 70 % (see Fig. 2). Rectangle, square, triangle, and trapezoid shapes had the most significant variation of surface roughness which corresponded to friction factors and in consequence Nusselt number increase. From a performance standpoint, the pentagonal shape was the best compared to a circular channel, reaching heat transfer close to the circular channel but with lower pressure drops. Of course, designers can trade pressure drop with the desire to maximize heat transfer if the requirements of the part allow for such a situation. An additional example of using the benefit of additive manufacturing is the work of Kirsch et al. [28], where micro-channels in sinusoidal shape were studied. Three configurations of wavy channels with wavelength (λ) compared to the length of the coupon (i.e., λ = 0.1 L, 0.2 L, and 0.4 L – see Fig. 3) and diameter of around 550 μm were manufactured using the DMLS process and INCO718 material. Tested Reynolds regimes were from 1000 to 10,000. Coupons with longer
wavelengths (i.e., λ = 0.2 L and 0.4 L) performed significantly better compared to shorter wavelength coupons (i.e., λ = 0.1 L). Friction factors for short wavelength coupons were consistently higher; however, heat transfer augmentation reached the same levels as shown in Fig. 4. Significant heat transfer boost compared to smooth straight channel with the same diameter calculated using the Dittus-Boelter equation was observed on all parts below Reynolds number equal 4000. The authors concluded that more studies should be performed to optimize channels from a cooling efficiency perspective and consider additive process parameters. Other types of 3D-printed mini/micro-channels are those that act as fluid passages with micro/mini pin fins located within the channels, additionally turbulating the flow and providing extra heat transfer area. Such channels were considered in the next paper [31]. It aimed to provide insights into the combined effects of high surface roughness with the complex flow structures characteristic of micro-pin fin arrays, which is a special consideration of channels created by metallic protrusions manufactured using the LPBF. Investigated samples showed extremely high roughness on some surfaces. Moreover, the surface roughness was found to be a function of the manufacturing process and design, i.e., pin spacing in the array and the shape of the pin itself, with more pins in the array resulting in higher surface roughness and more distorted pin shapes. The observed increase in friction factor for Reynolds number up to 20,000 between different test coupons with the same spanwise spacing, but different streamwise spacing, was attributed to the difference in surface roughness levels. When compared to their smooth pin fin array counterparts, additively manufactured arrays showed an increase in friction factor of 20–40 % (for a tighter spanwise spacing) and 60 % (for a wider spanwise spacing) over smooth arrays with the same spanwise spacing manufactured with traditional methods. In turn, the capabilities of the DMLS process to produce an array of micro-pin fins were assessed in [29] to understand how the 3D printing process affects the friction factor and heat transfer performance. Triangular, star, and spherical shaped pin fins placed in micro-channel test coupons were investigated for pressure loss and heat transfer at a range of Reynolds number of approx. 200–20,000. The influence of spacing, number of pin fins in the array, and pin fin geometry were tested. Unfortunately, the diameters of the pins varied, resulting in a change in the number of pins on the test coupon, which could significantly impact friction factors and final heat transfer augmentation, as reported by the research team. The summary of different pins’ performance can be seen in Fig. 5. Ferster et al. [29] concluded that more research needs to be conducted to fully understand additively manufactured pin fins, to optimize the types of finned heat exchangers and to find the effects of additively manufacturing process parameters on surface roughness and related micro-pin fin arrays performance. The ongoing development of additive methods allows more complex
