structures to be considered that can be used from a thermal performance perspective in novel heat exchangers or cooled turbine components. The research group from the University of Florence presented their results in [32]. The goal of the investigation was to characterize the thermal performance of coupons produced using the Direct Metal Laser Melting (DMLM) process, containing mini-channels acting as fluid passages with complex internal structures. Seven configurations of internal structures were prepared, i.e., pin fin, dimples, simple matrix, double matrix, and three configurations of Kagome lattice structure with different densities (i.e., low, medium, and high). Pin fin configuration is a solid pin staggered configuration. Dimples is a technique of augmentation of heat transfer where, on a targeted surface, valleys with the shape of a hemisphere are placed. Matrix coupon was designed by a combination of 45◦ turbulators on the bottom surface and on the upper surface negative to 45◦, causing turbulators to criss-cross in the middle surface of the coupon, creating a subchannel. Such structures enforce flow going through turbulated channel and suddenly does 90◦ turn, turbulating
flow structure and increasing heat transfer coefficient. Double matrix configuration incorporates additional rib between upper and lower turbulators, which, in consequence, causes denser subchannels. The last family of configurations is based on the Kagome structure, which is a three-dimensional structure formed by the vertices and edges of different densities. More details of simple and double matrix structures and the Kagome cell can be seen in Fig. 6. Tests were carried out for turbulent regimes with Reynolds numbers from 15,000 to 35,000. From the measured configurations, the lowest pressure drops were observed for the reference smooth coupon and dimples geometry. It was found that the friction factors were strongly dependent on the porosity of the test specimen and the wetted area. When the area was lower, then the pressure drop was also lower. For the lattice structures, trends showed that the higher the porosity, the higher the friction factors. The highest pressure drop, rising steeply with Reynolds numbers, was reported for double matrix configuration. According to the Reynolds analogy, heat transfer results are proportional to those representing the pressure drops. Higher average Nusselt number configurations show the dependence on the Reynolds number with a power law with a lower exponent. So here, the lowest heat transfer multiplier compared to the smooth surface was reported for the dimples test coupon and the highest for the double matrix. Fig. 7 shows the friction factor multipliers relative to the smooth mini-channel calculated using the Colebrook equation for appropriate hydraulic diameters vs heat transfer multipliers.
4. Conclusion
Additive manufacturing is a new method that significantly opens design space for engineers, allowing them to create normally nonproducible parts using conventional methods. Through the last few years, this method advanced significantly, but still, there are areas where cooperation between the research world and industry needs to be continued. This review summarized parameters of additive manufacturing that can influence cooled part dimensions, surface morphology, surface roughness, and, consequently, their impact on pressure drops and heat transfer augmentation. The main factors are
