building angle, the thickness of the building layer, and laser parameters, but also some not obvious factors like, e.g., placement of the part on the building plate, amount of support structures, or some specific dimensional relationships (e.g., a wall thickness of the part). 3D printing allows engineers to improve cooling efficiency for components on a microscale, which has been investigated in several works. The conclusion is that it is extremely important during the part’s design phase to consider possible shape changes and increased pressure drop due to surface finish to obtain final requirements. This summary discussed mini/micro-channels in different shapes and sizes, providing experts with guidance on what is achievable and what amount of trade needs to be taken. The current state of the art in terms of materials used to produce additively manufactured parts are alloys based on Co, Cr, and Al. The size of the channels is limited by the capabilities of current machines and is restricted to regions of 500 μm. There is still a niche in information and fluid flow and heat transfer research results for channels smaller than 500 μm – if such channels can even be produced using 3D printing methods, the question arises of what will be relative roughness and heat transfer augmentation? Moreover, can materials based on Cu have the same capability, or due to thermal conductivity production will be penalized with bigger uncertainties? Near years will probably help answer all these questions and allow engineers to produce more thermally efficient products.
